    solution_overview: "A library labels three books with the set $L = \\{\\text{Atlas}, \\text{Bramble}, \\text{Cinder}\\}$. Let $S = \\{\\text{Atlas}, \\text{Cinder}\\}$ be the books placed on a display shelf.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nThe elements of $L$ are the three individual titles Atlas, Bramble, and Cinder. The display set is $S=\\{\\text{Atlas},\\text{Cinder}\\}$, so it contains two of those titles.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nUse $x\\in A$ for an individual object in a set and $B\\subseteq A$ when every element of $B$ is in $A$. A set can be a subset without being an element.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) $\\text{Bramble} \\in L$.\n\nThe title Bramble is explicitly one of the three elements listed in $L$.\n\n**2.** (FALSE) $\\{\\text{Atlas}, \\text{Cinder}\\} \\in L$.\n\nThe elements of $L$ are individual book titles. The set $\\{\\text{Atlas},\\text{Cinder}\\}$ is a subset of $L$, not an element of it.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) $S \\subseteq L$.\n\nBoth books listed in $S$ also occur in $L$, so every element of $S$ belongs to $L$.\n\n**4.** (FALSE) $\\text{Atlas} \\subseteq S$.\n\nAtlas is a book title, not a set. The meaningful relation here is $\\text{Atlas}\\in S$, which is true.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) $|S| = 2$.\n\nThe display set lists two distinct titles: Atlas and Cinder.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=TRUE",
