    solution_overview: "Let A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. A partition of A is a collection of nonempty, pairwise disjoint subsets whose union is A.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nA valid partition needs blocks that are (1) nonempty, (2) pairwise disjoint, and (3) collectively cover every element of A exactly once. Check each candidate against all three: P={{1,2},{3,4},{5,6}} passes; Q fails disjointness (3 appears twice); R fails coverage (misses 6). There's no rule tying the number of blocks to |A|.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nUse the definitions of membership, subset, union, intersection, difference, complement, and power set as needed. Compute shared objects once.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) P = { {1,2}, {3,4}, {5,6} } is a partition of A.\n\nThe three blocks are nonempty, pairwise disjoint, and their union {1,2,3,4,5,6} equals A - all partition requirements are satisfied.\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Every partition of a set must have exactly the same number of blocks as the set has elements.\n\nA partition can have any number of blocks, from 1 (the whole set as a single block) up to n (all singletons) - there's no requirement that the number of blocks equals |A|, as (a) already shows with 3 blocks for 6 elements.\n\n**3.** (FALSE) Q = { {1,2,3}, {3,4,5,6} } is a partition of A.\n\nThe blocks {1,2,3} and {3,4,5,6} both contain the element 3, violating the requirement that blocks be pairwise disjoint.\n\n**4.** (FALSE) R = { {1,2}, {3,4}, {5} } is a partition of A.\n\nThe union of R's blocks is {1,2,3,4,5}, which misses the element 6 - the blocks don't cover all of A.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) A set with n ≥ 2 elements always has more than one possible partition.\n\nFor any n ≥ 2, at least two distinct partitions always exist - for example, the single block {A} itself, and the partition into all singletons - and these are different whenever n ≥ 2.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=FALSE, D=FALSE, E=TRUE",
