    solution_overview: "Let A = (0, 10] and B = [5, 15) be intervals of real numbers, treated as sets.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nCompute once: $A \\cap B$=[5,10] (tightest bounds from each side, both inclusive here); A B=(0,15)$\\cup$(no gap, open at both outer ends); A\\B=(0,5) (removing everything B also covers, i.e. everything ≥5). Test each statement - especially boundary points like x=5 and x=10 - directly against these three computed intervals.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nUse the definitions of membership, subset, union, intersection, difference, complement, and power set as needed. Compute shared objects once.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) $A \\cap B$ = [5, 10]\n\nA starts just above 0 and includes up to 10; B starts at 5 and goes up to just below 15. Their overlap is all x with 5 ≤ x ≤ 10, i.e. [5, 10].\n\n**2.** (FALSE) $A \\cup B$ = (0, 15]\n\n$Trap: 15 is not included in B (open at 15) and A doesn't reach 15 at all, so 15 A B. The correct \\notin \\cup union is (0, 15), not (0, 15].$\n\n**3.** (TRUE) $10 A \\cap B \\in$\n\n$10 belongs to A (its closed right endpoint) and to B (since 5 ≤ 10 < 15), so 10 A \\cap B. \\in$\n\n**4.** (FALSE) $5 A \\ B \\in$\n\nTrap: A \\ B removes from A everything also in B. Since B includes 5 (closed at 5), the value 5 is removed from A, so 5 A \\ B. The correct set is A \\ B = (0, 5). \\notin\n\n**5.** (FALSE) The statement \"x A x B\" is true for all x.$\\in \\Rightarrow$\\in$\n\nTrap: this implication fails for x = 1, which lies in A = (0,10] but not in B = [5,15) since 1 < 5. One counterexample is enough to make the universal implication false. Tier 3 - Advanced\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=FALSE",
