    solution_overview: "A science committee has members $C=\\{\\text{Inez},\\text{Jamal},\\text{Kira},\\text{Luca},\\text{Mei},\\text{Noah}\\}$. A subcommittee is any subset of $C$.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nThe committee has six distinct members. Count fixed-size subcommittees with binomial coefficients and all subsets with powers of 2.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nFor $n$ distinct elements, there are $2^n$ subsets and $\\binom{n}{k}$ subsets of size $k$. A proper subset excludes the full set itself.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) There are $\\binom{6}{3}=20$ three-person subcommittees.\n\nChoosing 3 members from 6 gives $\\binom{6}{3}=20$ possible groups.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) There are 15 two-person subcommittees.\n\nChoosing 2 members from 6 gives $\\binom{6}{2}=15$.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) There are 6 five-person subcommittees.\n\nA five-person group is formed by deciding who is left out, giving $\\binom{6}{5}=6$.\n\n**4.** (FALSE) There are 64 proper subsets of $C$.\n\nThere are $2^6=64$ subsets altogether, but the full set is not proper. Thus there are $63$ proper subsets.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) There are 32 subsets of $C$ that contain Inez.\n\nFix Inez in the group; each of the other five members may independently be included or excluded, giving $2^5=32$ subsets.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=TRUE",
