    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A) E is a proper subset of N.**  (true)\n\nEvery even number is a natural number, so E N; and 1 N but 1 E, so the inclusion is $strict \\subseteq \\in$\\notin$(proper).\n\nThe claim matches the computation above, so it is **true**.",
      "**B) Since E is a proper subset of N, E must have strictly fewer elements than N.**  (false)\n\nThis finite-set intuition fails for infinite sets: f(n)=2n is a bijection between N and E (part c), so despite being a proper subset, E has exactly the same cardinality as N, not fewer elements.\n\nThis is a common trap; the corrected reading shows the claim is **false**.",
      "**C) The function f(n) = 2n defines a one-to-one correspondence (bijection) between N and the odd natural numbers.**  (false)\n\nTrap: f(n) = 2n always produces an EVEN number, so its outputs land in E (the even naturals), never in the odd naturals - it cannot possibly be a bijection onto the odd numbers, since it never even reaches them.\n\nThis is a common trap; the corrected reading shows the claim is **false**.",
      "**D) The fact that E N yet has the same cardinality as N shows that the finite-set intuition “proper subset ⊊$\\Rightarrow fewer$ elements” does not carry over to infinite sets.**  (true)\n\nThis is precisely the classical insight (going back to Cantor and earlier to Galileo) that infinite sets can be in bijection with a proper subset of themselves, unlike finite sets.\n\nThe claim matches the computation above, so it is **true**.",
      "**E) Every infinite subset of N is equal to N itself (contains all the natural numbers).**  (false)\n\nTrap: this confuses \"same cardinality\" with \"being the same set.\" E itself is an infinite subset of N with the same cardinality as N, yet E ≠ N - E excludes every odd number, such as 1. Equal cardinality does not mean equal as sets.\n\nThis is a common trap; the corrected reading shows the claim is **false**.",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 29,
    solution_overview: "Let N = {1, 2, 3, ...} be the natural numbers and E = {2, 4, 6, ...} be the even natural numbers.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nf(n)=2n maps N bijectively onto E (every natural number n gives a unique even number 2n, and every even number is hit exactly once), so despite E being a proper subset of N (it excludes all odd numbers), E and N have the SAME cardinality - the classic infinite-set phenomenon where \"proper subset\" no longer implies \"fewer elements.\"\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nUse the definitions of membership, subset, union, intersection, difference, complement, and power set as needed. Compute shared objects once.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) E is a proper subset of N.\n\nEvery even number is a natural number, so E N; and 1 N but 1 E, so the inclusion is $strict \\subseteq \\in$\\notin$(proper).\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Since E is a proper subset of N, E must have strictly fewer elements than N.\n\nThis finite-set intuition fails for infinite sets: f(n)=2n is a bijection between N and E (part c), so despite being a proper subset, E has exactly the same cardinality as N, not fewer elements.\n\n**3.** (FALSE) The function f(n) = 2n defines a one-to-one correspondence (bijection) between N and the odd natural numbers.\n\nTrap: f(n) = 2n always produces an EVEN number, so its outputs land in E (the even naturals), never in the odd naturals - it cannot possibly be a bijection onto the odd numbers, since it never even reaches them.\n\n**4.** (TRUE) The fact that E N yet has the same cardinality as N shows that the finite-set intuition “proper subset ⊊$\\Rightarrow fewer$ elements” does not carry over to infinite sets.\n\nThis is precisely the classical insight (going back to Cantor and earlier to Galileo) that infinite sets can be in bijection with a proper subset of themselves, unlike finite sets.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) Every infinite subset of N is equal to N itself (contains all the natural numbers).\n\nTrap: this confuses \"same cardinality\" with \"being the same set.\" E itself is an infinite subset of N with the same cardinality as N, yet E ≠ N - E excludes every odd number, such as 1. Equal cardinality does not mean equal as sets.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=FALSE",
