    solution_overview: "$Let A = {x R : 1 \\in < x < 5} and B = {x R : x ≥ 3}.$\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nA = (1,5) and B = [3,∞) as intervals. Overlap them once: $A \\cap B$ = [3,5) (the tighter bound wins on each side), and A B = (1,∞) (no gap between the two, since A already reaches past 3). Neither interval contains $the \\cup other$ - x=2 is in A but not B, and x=10 is in B but not A.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nUse the definitions of membership, subset, union, intersection, difference, complement, and power set as needed. Compute shared objects once.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (FALSE) A B.$\\subseteq$\n\nA contains values like x = 2, which satisfies 1 < 2 < 5 but not 2 ≥ 3, so x = 2 A but x = 2 B - $this \\in \\notin$ counterexample shows A is not a subset of B.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) $A \\cap B$ = ${x R : 3 ≤ x < 5}$, i.e. the interval [3, 5).$\\in$\n\nCombining both conditions (1 < x < 5 and x ≥ 3), the binding restrictions become 3 ≤ x < 5, exactly the interval [3,5).\n\n**3.** (FALSE) $B A. \\subseteq$\n\n$B contains values like x = 10, which is not less than 5, so x = 10 B but x = 10 A - B is not a \\in \\notin subset of A.$\n\n**4.** (TRUE) $A \\cup B$ = ${x R : x > 1}$, i.e. the interval (1, ∞).$\\cup \\in$\n\nA covers (1,5) and B covers [3,∞); together every value greater than 1 is covered by one set or the other, with no gaps, giving exactly (1,∞).\n\n**5.** (TRUE) There exists a real number that belongs to A but not to B.\n\n$x = 2 satisfies 1 < 2 < 5 (so x A) but does not satisfy x ≥ 3 (so x B). \\in \\notin$\n\n**Answer.** A=FALSE, B=TRUE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=TRUE",
