    solution_overview: "Consider the universal claim: \"All prime numbers are odd,\" and the irrationality of √2.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nA single counterexample (like the even prime 2) is always enough to disprove a universal (\"for all\") claim - but a single confirming example never proves one. Proof by contradiction must assume the OPPOSITE of the target claim and derive an impossibility from THAT assumption, not from the claim itself.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) To disprove the universal statement \"all prime numbers are odd,\" it suffices to give one counterexample.\n\nA single counterexample is enough to disprove a universal (\"for all\") claim, since it directly contradicts \"every case satisfies the property.\"\n\n**2.** (TRUE) The number 2 is a valid counterexample to \"all prime numbers are odd.\"\n\n2 is prime and even, so it directly falsifies the claim that every prime is odd - a textbook counterexample.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) $Proving P Q by contradiction means assuming P is true and Q is false, then deriving a contradiction. \\Rightarrow$\n\nIn a proof by contradiction of P Q, one assumes the hypothesis P is true and the conclusion Q $is \\Rightarrow false$, then shows this combination leads to a logical contradiction, which forces P Q to be true.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**4.** (FALSE) A proof by contradiction that √2 is irrational begins by assuming that √2 IS irrational.\n\nTrap: this reverses the logic. A proof by contradiction of \"√2 is irrational\" must begin by assuming the OPPOSITE - that √2 IS rational - and then derive a contradiction from that assumption.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) If a statement is confirmed true for one specific example, this proves it is true for all cases.\n\nTrap: a single confirming example never proves a universal statement; it only shows the statement is not violated in that one case. Only a counterexample (a single failing case) can settle a universal claim - and only in the negative direction.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=FALSE",
