    solution_overview: "By De Morgan's law, NOT(on sale OR out of stock) = (NOT on sale) AND (NOT out of stock) - displayed only when BOTH conditions hold together, not OR (the classic De Morgan mistake). M (on sale) and N (out of stock) each trigger the OR, so neither is displayed; K satisfies both NOT\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nBy De Morgan's law, NOT(on sale OR out of stock) = (NOT on sale) AND (NOT out of stock) - displayed only when BOTH conditions hold together, not OR (the classic De Morgan mistake). M (on sale) and N (out of stock) each trigger the OR, so neither is displayed; K satisfies both NOT-conditions, so K is displayed.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (FALSE) Item M is displayed by the filter.\n\nM is on sale, so \"on sale OR out of stock\" is true, and the filter needs the negation of that to be true. Since it's false, M is not displayed.\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Item N is displayed by the filter.\n\nN is out of stock, so \"on sale OR out of stock\" is true (out-of-stock alone makes the OR true). The filter requires this to be false, so N is not displayed either.\n\n**3.** (FALSE) The filter condition \"NOT (on sale OR out of stock)\" is logically equivalent to \"NOT on sale OR NOT out of stock.\"\n\nThis is the classic De Morgan mistake: NOT(P OR Q) is equivalent to (NOT P) AND (NOT Q), not (NOT P) OR (NOT Q). The statement swaps AND for OR incorrectly.\n\n**4.** (TRUE) The filter condition is logically equivalent to \"NOT on sale AND NOT out of stock,\" meaning an item is displayed only if it is neither on sale nor out of stock.\n\nBy De Morgan's law, NOT(on sale OR out of stock) = (NOT on sale) AND (NOT out of stock) - an item is displayed exactly when both conditions (not on sale, and in stock) hold simultaneously.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) Item K is displayed by the filter.\n\nK is not on sale and is in stock, so both NOT on sale and NOT out of stock are true, satisfying the AND condition from part (d). K is displayed.\n\n**Answer.** A=FALSE, B=FALSE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=TRUE",
