    solution_overview: "Consider the (debatable) economic claim P Q:$\\Rightarrow$\"If inflation increases, then unemployment decreases.\" Each statement below is a proposed rewording - decide whether it is logically equivalent to P Q.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nFor P Q, only two reformulations are guaranteed equivalent: the $CONTRAPOSITIVE \\Rightarrow$($\\neg Q$\\neg P$) and phrasings that directly restate P Q as a sufficient/necessary condition. Any wording using \"only if\"$\\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$ attached to Q, or \"for Q to happen, P must happen,\" actually expresses the CONVERSE (Q P) instead - $a \\Rightarrow different$, non-equivalent claim.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (FALSE) $\"For unemployment to decrease, inflation must increase\" is equivalent to P Q. \\Rightarrow$\n\nTrap: \"For Q to happen, P must happen\" translates to Q P - this is the CONVERSE of $the \\Rightarrow original$ implication, not the original itself, and the converse is not logically equivalent to it.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) $\"A sufficient condition for unemployment to decrease is that inflation increases\" is equivalent to P Q. \\Rightarrow$\n\n$\"A sufficient condition for Q is P\" is precisely the verbal definition of P Q, so this rewording is \\Rightarrow equivalent.$\n\n**3.** (FALSE) $\"Unemployment can only decrease if inflation increases\" is equivalent to P Q. \\Rightarrow$\n\nTrap: \"Q only if P\" also translates to Q P (the converse), the same trap as statement (a) $worded \\Rightarrow differently$ - it is NOT equivalent to P Q.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**4.** (TRUE) $\"If unemployment does not decrease, then inflation does not increase\" is equivalent to P Q. \\Rightarrow$\n\n\"$If \\neg Q then \\neg P$\" is exactly the $contrapositive \\neg Q$\\neg P$, which is always logically equivalent to $the \\Rightarrow original$ implication P Q.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**5.** (TRUE) $\"A necessary condition for inflation to increase is that unemployment decreases\" is equivalent to P Q. \\Rightarrow$\n\n$\"A necessary condition for P is Q\" translates to P Q (Q must hold whenever P holds) - this is \\Rightarrow exactly the original statement.$\n\n**Answer.** A=FALSE, B=TRUE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=TRUE",
