    solution_overview: "Without a waiver, both GPA≥3.5 AND credits≥60 are required; with a waiver, only GPA≥3.5 matters (credits become irrelevant). M (no waiver, 50 credits) fails the credit requirement; N (has waiver, GPA 3.6) only needs to clear 3.5, which it does. The GPA threshold is NEVER waived -\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nWithout a waiver, both GPA≥3.5 AND credits≥60 are required; with a waiver, only GPA≥3.5 matters (credits become irrelevant). M (no waiver, 50 credits) fails the credit requirement; N (has waiver, GPA 3.6) only needs to clear 3.5, which it does. The GPA threshold is NEVER waived - only the credit-hour part is.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (FALSE) Student M is eligible for the scholarship.\n\nM has no waiver, so both the GPA and credit-hour conditions are required; M's 50 credit hours fall short of the required 60, so M is not eligible.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) Student N is eligible for the scholarship.\n\nN has a waiver, so only the GPA condition applies; N's GPA of 3.6 meets the required 3.5, so N is eligible despite having only 45 credit hours.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) If Student M were to receive a Dean's waiver (keeping the same GPA and credit hours), M would become eligible.\n\nWith a waiver, only the GPA condition matters, and M's GPA (3.7) already satisfies the 3.5 threshold.\n\n**4.** (FALSE) The Dean's waiver, by itself, is sufficient for eligibility, regardless of GPA.\n\nThe waiver only removes the credit-hour requirement; the GPA condition of at least 3.5 must still be independently satisfied.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) It is possible for a student with a GPA below 3.5 to be eligible, provided they have a waiver and sufficient credit hours.\n\nThe GPA condition is never waived by this rule - the waiver only ever concerns the credit-hour requirement, so a GPA below 3.5 always fails regardless of waiver or credit hours.\n\n**Answer.** A=FALSE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=FALSE",
