    difficulty_level: "4/5",
    sort_order: 27,
    solution_overview: "An election law states P Q:$\\Rightarrow$\"If a person is a citizen, they are eligible to vote.\"\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nP Q guarantees its contrapositive ($\\neg Q$\\neg P$) but not the converse (Q P) - some non-$citizens \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$\\Rightarrow can$ be granted voting rights. The converse and inverse are always equivalent to EACH OTHER regardless of context, since each is literally the other's contrapositive.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) The negation of this law is: \"A person is a citizen and is not eligible to vote.\"\n\nThe negation of P Q is $P \\neg Q$ - a citizen who is not eligible to vote.$\\Rightarrow \\land$\n\n**2.** (TRUE) $John is not eligible to vote (\\neg Q); by the contrapositive, we may conclude John is not a citizen (\\neg P). This reasoning is logically valid.$\n\n$\"\\neg Q, therefore \\neg P\" is precisely the contrapositive \\neg Q \\neg P, which is valid whenever the original law \\Rightarrow is accepted as true.$\n\n**3.** (FALSE) Maria is a citizen who chooses not to register and therefore does not cast a vote in one election. This is a counterexample disproving the law.\n\nTrap: the law is about eligibility, not actual voting. Maria being eligible but choosing not to vote does not make Q (\"eligible to vote\") false - she conflates \"not voting\" with \"not eligible,\" so this is not a genuine counterexample.\n\n**4.** (TRUE) The converse, \"If a person is eligible to vote, they are a citizen,\" could be false in a country that grants voting rights to certain non-citizen long-term residents.\n\nThe converse Q P need not hold: some jurisdictions extend voting eligibility to certain non-$\\Rightarrow citizens$, giving Q true and P false.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) The inverse, \"If a person is not a citizen, they are not eligible to vote,\" always has the same truth value as the converse.\n\nThis is a structural fact independent of context: the inverse ($\\neg P$\\neg Q$) and the converse (Q P) $are \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$ always logically equivalent to each other, since one is the contrapositive of the other.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=TRUE",
