    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 29,
    solution_overview: "Consider the claim: \"The sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational,\" and the classical proof that there is no largest prime number.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\n√2+(-√2)=0 is a single counterexample disproving \"sum of two irrationals is always irrational\" - one case is always enough to kill a universal claim, never enough to prove one. A genuine contradiction proof of \"no largest prime exists\" must assume the OPPOSITE (a largest prime p DOES exist) and derive an impossibility from that.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) √2 + (-√2) = 0 is a valid counterexample to \"the sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational,\" since √2 and -√2 are irrational but their sum, 0, is rational\n\n√2 and -√2 are both irrational, yet their sum is 0, which is rational - exactly one counterexample is needed to break the universal claim.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) Finding this one counterexample is enough to show the claim is not a true universal statement\n\nA single counterexample is sufficient to disprove a \"for all\" statement, since it directly demonstrates a case where the property fails.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) A proof by contradiction that there is no largest prime begins by assuming there IS a largest prime, say p, and then derives a contradiction\n\nThis is the standard structure of Euclid's classical proof: assume the opposite of what we want to prove (that a largest prime p exists), then construct a contradiction (e.g. a new prime larger than p).\n\n**4.** (FALSE) $To prove a universal statement \" x, P(x)\" is true, it suffices to check it for x = 1 and x = 2 \\forall$\n\nTrap: checking a finite number of cases, however many, never proves a universal statement about infinitely many (or even just many) values - a single untested case could still be a counterexample.\n\n**5.** (TRUE) $To prove an existential statement \" x, P(x)\" is true, it suffices to exhibit just one value of x for which P(x) \\exists holds$\n\nUnlike universal statements, existential statements only claim that at least one example exists - so producing a single valid example is a complete proof.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=TRUE",
