    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 30,
    solution_overview: "A biology rule states P Q:$\\Rightarrow$\"If an animal is a fish, then it lives in water.\"\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nFrom P Q (\"fish lives in water\"): using the CONVERSE (Q P) is a fallacy (dolphins $disprove \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$\\Rightarrow it$); using the INVERSE ($\\neg P$\\neg Q$) is also a fallacy (water snakes disprove it); only the $CONTRAPOSITIVE \\Rightarrow$($\\neg Q$\\neg P$) is guaranteed valid.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nRecall: $p \\Rightarrow q$ is false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false; $\\neg(p \\land q) \\equiv \\neg p \\lor \\neg q$; the converse of an implication is not automatic.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) \"This dolphin lives in water, so it must be a fish\" uses the converse of the rule and is an invalid argument.\n\nThe argument has form Q P, the converse of P Q. It's invalid, and dolphins prove it: they live $in \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$ water yet are mammals, not fish.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) \"This lizard does not live in water, so it is not a fish\" uses the contrapositive of the rule and is a valid argument.\n\n$This argument has form \\neg Q \\neg P, exactly the contrapositive of P Q, always equivalent to the \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow original - so the reasoning is valid.$\n\n**3.** (FALSE) \"This snake is not a fish, so it does not live in water\" uses the inverse of the rule, and inverse-based reasoning is always guaranteed to be valid.\n\n$Trap: this has form \\neg P \\neg Q (the inverse), not guaranteed by the original. Water snakes break it: \\Rightarrow not fish, yet they live in water.$\n\n**4.** (TRUE) The negation of the broader claim \"All fish live in water\" is \"There exists a fish that does not live in water.\"\n\n$\\neg$( x, P(x)) ≡ x,$\\neg P$(x). Applied here, this gives exactly \"there exists a fish that does not live $in \\forall \\exists$ water\" - correctly formed regardless of its truth.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) The fact that dolphins live in water without being fish is, by itself, a valid counterexample disproving \"All fish live in water.\"\n\nTrap: a counterexample to \"all fish live in water\" must be a fish that does NOT live in water. A dolphin (not a fish) living in water is irrelevant to this claim; it would only bear on the reversed statement \"everything in water is a fish.\"\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=FALSE",
