    solution_overview: "The exception is conjunctive: BOTH written approval AND annual servicing are needed to keep a commercially-used product's warranty valid; missing either one alone lets the base \"void if commercial use\" rule apply. Company Z has approval but no servicing, so the exception fails an\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nThe exception is conjunctive: BOTH written approval AND annual servicing are needed to keep a commercially-used product's warranty valid; missing either one alone lets the base \"void if commercial use\" rule apply. Company Z has approval but no servicing, so the exception fails and the warranty is void.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nNegation switches quantifiers: $\\neg \\forall x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\exists x\\, \\neg P(x)$ and $\\neg \\exists x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\forall x\\, \\neg P(x)$. For deduction puzzles, exhaust consistent assignments.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) Company Z's warranty is void.\n\nZ has approval but has not serviced the product annually, so the exception's full requirement (approval AND servicing) is not satisfied, and the commercial-use voiding rule applies.\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Having written approval from the manufacturer alone is sufficient to keep the warranty valid despite commercial use.\n\nAs shown in (a), approval alone did not save Z's warranty - annual servicing is also required by the exception clause.\n\n**3.** (TRUE) The rule's exception requires both approval AND annual servicing to keep the warranty valid under commercial use - satisfying only one of the two is not enough.\n\nThis is a direct restatement of the clause's structure: the exception is conjunctive (approval AND servicing), not satisfied by either alone.\n\n**4.** (FALSE) If a company uses the product commercially without any manufacturer approval at all, the warranty could still remain valid provided they service it annually.\n\nTrap: without approval, the exception's condition (approval AND servicing) automatically fails regardless of servicing - servicing alone cannot rescue the warranty. The base rule (void for commercial use) applies unconditionally here.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) If Company Z had never used the product commercially at all, the annual-servicing requirement mentioned in the clause would still apply to it.\n\nThe servicing requirement is specifically part of the exception clause for commercial use; if there's no commercial use at all, this clause and its servicing condition are simply not triggered.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=FALSE",
