    solution_overview: "Factor each pair to check for shared primes: 15=$3 \\times 5$, 28=2^$2 \\times 7$ (no shared prime gcd=1,$\\Rightarrow coprime$); 24=2^$3 \\times 3$, 36=2^$2 \\times 3$^2 (share both 2 and 3 gcd=12, NOT coprime). Coprimality only requires no $SHARED \\Rightarrow prime$ factor - individual numbers can still be composite, and any two even numbers au\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nFactor each pair to check for shared primes: 15=$3 \\times 5$, 28=2^$2 \\times 7$ (no shared prime gcd=1,$\\Rightarrow coprime$); 24=2^$3 \\times 3$, 36=2^$2 \\times 3$^2 (share both 2 and 3 gcd=12, NOT coprime). Coprimality only requires no $SHARED \\Rightarrow prime$ factor - individual numbers can still be composite, and any two even numbers automatically share the prime 2.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nNegation switches quantifiers: $\\neg \\forall x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\exists x\\, \\neg P(x)$ and $\\neg \\exists x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\forall x\\, \\neg P(x)$. For deduction puzzles, exhaust consistent assignments.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) Pair 1 (15 and 28) is coprime, so this pair will wear evenly.\n\n$15 = 3 \\times 5 and 28 = 2^2 \\times 7 share no common prime factor, so gcd(15, 28) = 1 - the pair is coprime and will wear evenly.$\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Pair 2 (24 and 36) is coprime.\n\n$24 = 2^3 \\times 3 and 36 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 both share the prime factors 2 and 3, so gcd(24, 36) = 12 ≠ 1 - the pair is not coprime.$\n\n**3.** (TRUE) The correct negation of \"m and n are coprime\" is: \"there exists a prime p such that p divides m and p divides n.\"\n\nNegating \"for every prime p, not (p|m and p|n)\" flips the universal quantifier to existential and negates the inner statement, giving exactly \"there exists a prime p such that p divides both m and n.\"\n\n**4.** (TRUE) If m and n are coprime and both are greater than 1, then m and n cannot both be even numbers.\n\nIf both m and n were even, then 2 would divide both, so 2 would be a common prime factor - contradicting gcd(m, n) = 1. So coprime numbers cannot both be even.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) Every coprime pair of integers greater than 1 must include at least one prime number.\n\nPair 1 (15 and 28) is coprime by part (a), yet neither 15 (= $3 \\times 5$) nor 28 (= 2^$2 \\times 7$) is itself prime. A coprime pair only needs to share no common prime factor - each number individually can still be composite.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=TRUE, D=TRUE, E=FALSE",
