    difficulty_level: "4/5",
    sort_order: 16,
    solution_overview: "$In number theory: P Q: \\Rightarrow \"If n is divisible by 6, then n is divisible by 3.\"$\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\n\"n div by 6 n div by 3\" is TRUE for every integer (since 6=$2 \\times 3$), making its $negation \\Rightarrow impossible$ for ALL n, and its contrapositive TRUE for every n too - a statement's own truth is completely independent of whether its CONVERSE (\"n div by 3 n div by 6,\" false via n=9) happens to hold.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nNegation switches quantifiers: $\\neg \\forall x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\exists x\\, \\neg P(x)$ and $\\neg \\exists x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\forall x\\, \\neg P(x)$. For deduction puzzles, exhaust consistent assignments.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (FALSE) The negation, \"n is divisible by 6 and n is not divisible by 3,\" describes a situation that occurs for infinitely many integers n.\n\nTrap: since 6 = $2 \\times 3$, every multiple of 6 is automatically a multiple of 3, so the situation \"divisible by 6 but not by 3\" is impossible - the negation is unsatisfiable for EVERY integer n, not true for infinitely many.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) The converse, \"If n is divisible by 3, then n is divisible by 6,\" is false - n = 9 is a counterexample (divisible by 3, not by 6).\n\n$9 is divisible by 3 but not by 6, directly breaking the converse Q P. \\Rightarrow$\n\n**3.** (TRUE) The inverse, \"If n is not divisible by 6, then n is not divisible by 3,\" is logically equivalent to the converse, so it is also false, with n = 9 again serving as its counterexample.\n\nThe $inverse \\neg P$\\neg Q$ is always logically equivalent to the converse Q P, so since the $converse \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$ fails at n = 9 (3 | 9 but 6 9), the inverse fails there too: n = 9 is not divisible by 6 ($\\neg P$ true) yet is divisible by 3 ∤ ($\\neg Q$ false).\n\n**4.** (FALSE) The contrapositive, \"If n is not divisible by 3, then n is not divisible by 6,\" is false for some integers n.\n\nTrap: the contrapositive is always equivalent to the original statement, and the original is materially true (any multiple of 6 is a multiple of 3), so the contrapositive is actually true for EVERY integer n, not false for some.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) $Since the converse is false, the original statement \"n divisible by 6 n divisible by 3\" must also be false. \\Rightarrow$\n\nTrap: a statement's truth is independent of its converse's truth. The original \"6 3\" is true $precisely \\Rightarrow because$ 6 = $2 \\times 3$, regardless of the converse being false - confusing the two is a very common error.\n\n**Answer.** A=FALSE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=FALSE, E=FALSE",
