      "**B) The converse, \"If a sequence is bounded, then it converges,\" is false - the sequence (-1)ⁿ (that is, -1, 1, -1, 1,...) is bounded but does not converge, serving as a counterexample.**  (true)\n\n(-1)ⁿ never settles on a single value (it oscillates forever between -1 and 1), so it does not converge, yet it stays within [-1, 1], so it is bounded - Q true (bounded), P false (doesn't converge), breaking Q P.$\\Rightarrow$\n\nThe claim matches the computation above, so it is **true**.",
