    solution_overview: "A real-analysis theorem states P Q:$\\Rightarrow$\"If a sequence converges, then it is bounded.\" (This is a proven true theorem.)\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\n\"Convergent bounded\" is a proven true theorem, so its negation is impossible and $its \\Rightarrow contrapositive$ is true too - completely independent of the fact that its CONVERSE (\"bounded convergent\") $is \\Rightarrow false$, disproven by the oscillating sequence (-1)ⁿ. The inverse shares the converse's fate (false) via the same counterexample, since inverse≡converse always.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nNegation switches quantifiers: $\\neg \\forall x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\exists x\\, \\neg P(x)$ and $\\neg \\exists x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\forall x\\, \\neg P(x)$. For deduction puzzles, exhaust consistent assignments.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) The negation, \"a sequence converges and is not bounded,\" describes an impossible situation, since the theorem holds for every sequence.\n\nSince the theorem is proven true for every sequence, no sequence can simultaneously converge and be unbounded - the negation describes an empty, impossible case.\n\n**2.** (TRUE) The converse, \"If a sequence is bounded, then it converges,\" is false - the sequence (-1)ⁿ (that is, -1, 1, -1, 1,...) is bounded but does not converge, serving as a counterexample.\n\n(-1)ⁿ never settles on a single value (it oscillates forever between -1 and 1), so it does not converge, yet it stays within [-1, 1], so it is bounded - Q true (bounded), P false (doesn't converge), breaking Q P.$\\Rightarrow$\n\n**3.** (TRUE) The inverse, \"If a sequence does not converge, then it is not bounded,\" is logically equivalent to the converse, and is therefore also false, using the same sequence (-1)ⁿ as its counterexample.\n\nThe $inverse \\neg P$\\neg Q$ is always equivalent to the converse Q P. Since (-1)ⁿ makes the $converse \\Rightarrow \\Rightarrow$ false (bounded but not convergent), it makes the inverse false too:$\\neg P$ is true (doesn't converge) $but \\neg Q$ is false (it IS bounded).\n\n**4.** (TRUE) The contrapositive, \"If a sequence is not bounded, then it does not converge,\" is true, and is in fact the version most commonly used to prove specific unbounded sequences (like n, 2n, n^2) do not converge.\n\nThe contrapositive is always equivalent to the original theorem, which is true, so the contrapositive is true too - and indeed it's the standard tool for proving unboundedness implies divergence.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) Because the converse is false, the original theorem itself must also be false.\n\nTrap: a statement's truth does not depend on its converse's truth. The original theorem (\"convergent bounded\") is a proven true fact, entirely independent of the converse being false - this is the same $confusion \\Rightarrow seen$ with the divisibility and sports examples earlier.\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=TRUE, C=TRUE, D=TRUE, E=FALSE",
