    solution_overview: "Chaining rule 4 by contrapositive gives $Owen \\neg Quinn$. Rule 2 (Owen Priya) blocks both $from \\Rightarrow \\lor$ sitting out at once, ruling out \"Quinn cooks alone.\" Setting Owen=out, Priya=in, Quinn=in (forced by rule 3) satisfies every rule, showing Owen is never strictly required.\n\n**Part 1: Setup.**\n\nChaining rule 4 by contrapositive gives $Owen \\neg Quinn$. Rule 2 (Owen Priya) blocks both $from \\Rightarrow \\lor$ sitting out at once, ruling out \"Quinn cooks alone.\" Setting Owen=out, Priya=in, Quinn=in (forced by rule 3) satisfies every rule, showing Owen is never strictly required.\n\n**Part 2: Key facts.**\n\nNegation switches quantifiers: $\\neg \\forall x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\exists x\\, \\neg P(x)$ and $\\neg \\exists x\\, P(x) \\equiv \\forall x\\, \\neg P(x)$. For deduction puzzles, exhaust consistent assignments.\n\n**Part 3: Solve.**\n\n**1.** (TRUE) If Owen cooks, then Quinn does not cook.\n\n$This is the contrapositive of rule 4 (Quinn → \\neg Owen becomes Owen → \\neg Quinn) - always guaranteed.$\n\n**2.** (FALSE) Owen and Priya can both cook tonight.\n\nTrap: rule 1 directly forbids this - Owen cooking forces Priya not to cook.\n\n**3.** (FALSE) It is possible that neither Owen nor Priya cooks, and Quinn cooks alone.\n\nTrap: rule 2 requires at least one of Owen or Priya to cook. If both sit out, rule 2 is violated outright - Quinn cooking \"alone\" (with neither Owen nor Priya) is never a valid scenario, no matter what Quinn does.\n\n**4.** (TRUE) Priya cooking guarantees that Quinn also cooks.\n\nThis is simply rule 3 restated directly - always guaranteed by assumption.\n\n**5.** (FALSE) Owen must cook tonight.\n\nTrap: setting Owen out, Priya in, Quinn in satisfies every rule (rule 2 via Priya, rule 3 forces Quinn in, rule 4 is consistent since Owen is out, rule 1 is vacuous) - a fully valid scenario where Owen never cooks at all. Hard Difficulty\n\n**Answer.** A=TRUE, B=FALSE, C=FALSE, D=TRUE, E=FALSE",
